// fire — the first kernel whose appearance is not a curve over age.
//
// It writes no colour. It writes a POSITION IN AN APPEARANCE MANIFOLD:
// three state channels the renderer reads an RBF set at (funideas §9).
// Age is still in here — it is what `perish` reads — but nothing about
// how the row LOOKS comes from it. What the row looks like is wherever
// its state got to, and the state is pushed around by the world.
//
//   row.u0  cooled   0 white-hot ... 1 cold
//   row.u1  sooted   0 clean ....... 1 all carbon
//   row.u2  thinned  0 dense ....... 1 blown thin
//
// All three are born at ZERO, which is not a coincidence: a spawn
// zeroes a row's user channels, so the axes are spelled as how much has
// HAPPENED to the row, never as what it has left. A newborn has no
// history, and (0, 0, 0) is the flame.
//
// Every line is a `relax` — the step toward a target at a rate per
// SECOND, which saturates, so no influence can drive a channel past its
// target and no clamp is needed to say so. They are all `write … add`,
// and a row's queued adds land on the live value in order, so the
// influences SUM. That is the whole trick. A second cooling term is not
// a special case of the first, it is another force on the same channel.
//
// The knobs are rates per second (`relax` eats the fed dt). The first
// draft of this kernel spelled each line `| mul -1 | add 1 | mul <rate>`
// and the rates were per TICK — correct at one dt and quietly wrong at
// every other. That kernel is what `relax` was built for.

spawn
gravity plane.drift.@self.gravity
collide | stick
perish

// -- cooled ------------------------------------------------------------
// It cools on its own,
row.u0 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.cool | write row.u0 add
// faster against something it landed on (the cold plate),
row.u0 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.plunge | mul row.stuck | write row.u0 add
// and faster once it is thin enough for the air to get at it.
row.u0 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.chill | mul row.u2 | write row.u0 add

// -- sooted ------------------------------------------------------------
// Carbon accumulates as it burns,
row.u1 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.smoke | write row.u1 add
// and a quench dumps it: incomplete combustion is what a soot mark IS.
row.u1 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.quench | mul row.stuck | write row.u1 add

// -- thinned -----------------------------------------------------------
// It spreads as it goes. (The knob is `thin`, not `spread`: a kernel's
// knobs and the SPRAY's own knobs share one namespace, so naming this
// one `spread` silently retuned the launch cone to 0.013 cells/s — the
// mount line still printed the 1.6 the flag asked for, and every row
// went straight up in a pencil. Named for the channel it drives.)
row.u2 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.thin | write row.u2 add
// A stuck row is pulled back toward dense, so a soot mark stays a mark.
// It does not reach zero: `thin` is not gated on being free, so the two
// lines BALANCE at thin/(thin + settle) rather than cancelling. Gating
// it would want two saturating factors in one flow, which a row cannot
// spell today.
row.u2 | relax 0 plane.drift.@self.settle | mul row.stuck | write row.u2 add

// The row's SIZE is state too, not a curve: a thin row is a wide one.
// (funideas §9 — the particle morphs its whole rendering model.)
row.u2 | mul plane.drift.@self.puff | add plane.drift.@self.grain | write row.size
